# Line endings, and why this file has to exist.
#
# Git for Windows installs with `core.autocrlf=true`. Without a rule here, a
# clone on Windows rewrites every text file in the working tree to CRLF —
# including `docker-entrypoint.sh`, which the Dockerfile COPYs into the image
# and runs as the entrypoint. A CRLF shebang is not a cosmetic difference: the
# kernel reads the carriage return as part of the interpreter's name, and the
# container exits immediately with `bad interpreter`. The app never starts, and
# nothing in the failure names the cause.
#
# `Requirements` in the README names no operating system, and CI covers
# linux/amd64 and linux/arm64 — so nothing else in this repository would catch
# it, and the one person it happens to is a first-time Windows operator with no
# reason to suspect their clone.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises on commit and checks out LF everywhere, which is
# right for a project whose only execution target is a Linux container: there is
# no file here that a Windows program needs CRLF to read, and the editors that
# once required it have not for a decade.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit for the executable ones, so a future `* -text` or a per-machine
# autocrlf cannot quietly take them back. These are the files whose corruption
# stops the container rather than showing up in a diff.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.mjs       text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
docker-entrypoint.sh text eol=lf

# Binary, so Git never tries to translate them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
